1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:14,001
Reach across time and see yourself.

2
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:19,000
You stand tall against terrifying enemies.

3
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:25,060
You build masterpieces and break down barriers.

4
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:29,560
You rush into the future and meet your past.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,480
For every time, at every moment, the official network of every millennium.

6
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:38,620
The History Channel.

7
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:41,540
Where the past comes alive.

8
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:48,920
The Internet is the defining technology of our time.

9
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:54,000
An historic revolution in communications for the human race that is changing the world.

10
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,380
Well, the Internet is the next big thing.

11
00:00:56,381 --> 00:01:00,620
It is on a par with the wheel and fire and language and the printing press.

12
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:06,660
The Internet enables millions of people with personal computers to communicate and connect.

13
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:08,160
It's email.

14
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:09,500
You've got mail.

15
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:12,700
It's newsgroups and chat rooms.

16
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:17,180
It's also the world's largest marketplace and shopping mall.

17
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:25,000
And it's a link to websites the world over with information for every aspect of life

18
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:27,080
and even the life after.

19
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:33,560
This is, in fact, the telecommunications infrastructure for the 21st century.

20
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:36,080
But where did the Internet come from?

21
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:38,880
How did it get started?

22
00:01:40,140 --> 00:01:44,020
And who built this essential tool and playful toy to millions?

23
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:46,520
And where is it going?

24
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:02,500
This is the age of the Internet, a global network of computer-based communication.

25
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:14,300
At the turn of the 21st century, millions of people around the world are now online every day to exchange messages, conduct business, meet people, and find information.

26
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:19,720
Free from the limits of time, distance, and even tyranny.

27
00:02:19,721 --> 00:02:28,320
Computer communication can also be a tool of war to manage materiel and manpower,

28
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:31,840
as the UN coalition did in the war against Saddam Hussein.

29
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:39,000
If you knew where things were and you knew what was available better than your opponent did,

30
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:41,400
then you could organize your offensive.

31
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:45,560
And I think we learned that lesson pretty clearly in the 1992 Gulf War,

32
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:48,620
where we clearly knew where things were better than our opponents did.

33
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:54,720
But the Internet is also a vital information link.

34
00:02:54,721 --> 00:03:00,720
During the Kosovo crisis, it carried uncensored news past state-owned newspapers, television and radio,

35
00:03:00,721 --> 00:03:03,720
with the real story of the Balkan tragedy.

36
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:12,720
Be it peace or war, the worldwide use of the Internet is practically indispensable.

37
00:03:12,721 --> 00:03:17,720
Yet its origins date back only to the 1950s.

38
00:03:17,721 --> 00:03:24,720
No invention in history has grown so fast to touch so many lives.

39
00:03:24,721 --> 00:03:30,720
It's something that we didn't see with the telephone,

40
00:03:30,721 --> 00:03:33,720
with the automobile, or with the printing press.

41
00:03:33,721 --> 00:03:38,720
This is an absolutely incredible communications tool,

42
00:03:38,721 --> 00:03:45,720
and it gives us instant access to things happening everywhere.

43
00:03:45,721 --> 00:03:53,558
In scope, more email is exchanged on the Internet today than first-class letters delivered by the U.S.

44
00:03:53,559 --> 00:03:54,480
Post Office.

45
00:03:57,180 --> 00:04:02,860
In financial importance, the Internet generated $301 billion in business

46
00:04:02,861 --> 00:04:08,200
and was responsible for one-third of the total U.S. economic growth in 1998.

47
00:04:08,201 --> 00:04:12,360
It is a key component of our culture and civilization.

48
00:04:12,361 --> 00:04:16,360
and yet no one really controls it.

49
00:04:16,361 --> 00:04:21,360
Things that matter the most usually don't have anybody in charge.

50
00:04:21,361 --> 00:04:27,599
The seminal idea behind the Internet was envisioned by a psychologist named J.C.R.

51
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:28,360
Licklider,

52
00:04:28,361 --> 00:04:33,360
a professor at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in the 1950s.

53
00:04:33,361 --> 00:04:37,360
Licklider's vision was that computers would allow you to communicate better

54
00:04:37,361 --> 00:04:39,360
and that would allow you to connect all the computers together

55
00:04:39,361 --> 00:04:43,760
computers together so that they might be more useful in the sharing of information among people.

56
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:49,600
Computer communication was a flight of imagination far beyond anything at the movies.

57
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:57,440
Computers were portrayed as sci-fi props like electronic brains alongside robots,

58
00:04:57,441 --> 00:04:59,120
ray guns and flying saucers.

59
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:08,240
In fact, the idea of a worldwide communications network of computers was far more imaginative

60
00:05:08,241 --> 00:05:10,040
than anything Hollywood came up with.

61
00:05:12,580 --> 00:05:16,920
After all, the first computers were monstrously large and expensive devices

62
00:05:16,921 --> 00:05:18,780
that were never intended to communicate.

63
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:21,280
All the computers were as big as a room.

64
00:05:21,560 --> 00:05:25,220
You were lucky to have one in a city or maybe one on a campus

65
00:05:25,221 --> 00:05:27,320
or, if you were lucky, one in a room.

66
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:31,780
They could have remained stand-alone computing machines for a long time,

67
00:05:32,120 --> 00:05:35,580
but a frightening display of Soviet scientific superiority

68
00:05:35,581 --> 00:05:42,580
during the Cold War spurred them into teamwork.

69
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:48,580
In October 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik.

70
00:05:48,581 --> 00:05:52,580
To catch up and hopefully surpass the Soviets in space technology,

71
00:05:52,581 --> 00:05:55,580
President Eisenhower created the bureau at the Defense Department

72
00:05:55,581 --> 00:06:00,580
called ARPA, the Advanced Research Projects Agency.

73
00:06:00,581 --> 00:06:02,580
It was in charge of the space program,

74
00:06:02,581 --> 00:06:05,580
which then included computer science.

75
00:06:05,581 --> 00:06:09,580
But when the space program got its own agency, NASA,

76
00:06:09,581 --> 00:06:12,580
computer research fell into relative obscurity.

77
00:06:12,581 --> 00:06:15,580
America turned its attention to conquering the stars

78
00:06:15,581 --> 00:06:18,580
and beating the Russians in space.

79
00:06:18,581 --> 00:06:21,580
I believe that this nation should commit itself

80
00:06:21,581 --> 00:06:24,580
to achieving the goal before this decade is out

81
00:06:24,581 --> 00:06:26,580
of landing a man on the moon

82
00:06:26,581 --> 00:06:29,580
and returning him safely to the Earth.

83
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:43,180
While America gave birth to a space program that grew from a crawl to a run by presidential

84
00:06:43,181 --> 00:06:48,900
edict, computer communication was in the embryonic stage, but it was making progress.

85
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:56,640
At MIT, a graduate student named Leonard Kleinrock began applying queuing theory to data transmission.

86
00:06:57,540 --> 00:07:02,540
Cueing theory is a fantastic tool for evaluating computer and communication systems.

87
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:05,040
You know, a queue is a very simple structure.

88
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:09,140
It's simply a system to which you arrive, hang around a while, and leave.

89
00:07:10,140 --> 00:07:12,460
Like letters going through post offices,

90
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:16,560
Kleinrock showed how packages of data would queue or line up

91
00:07:16,561 --> 00:07:19,980
at the nodes of a communications network as they transited through.

92
00:07:20,720 --> 00:07:24,240
This way, he could determine the overall speed of the network.

93
00:07:24,241 --> 00:07:31,240
He then showed how to speed up the network by implementing two techniques, demand access

94
00:07:31,241 --> 00:07:32,240
and distributed control.

95
00:07:32,241 --> 00:07:38,480
So the point of demand access, you don't get it till you need it, is the key idea for effective

96
00:07:38,481 --> 00:07:40,480
resource sharing with data networks.

97
00:07:40,481 --> 00:07:46,480
Now the second principle that I uncovered was the idea that to use distributed control

98
00:07:46,481 --> 00:07:53,240
in a network, no one switch device authority should control the entire network.

99
00:07:53,241 --> 00:07:56,160
Rather, every switch should share in that control.

100
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:58,320
It should decide the routing procedure,

101
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:00,860
it should decide how much traffic is allowed in,

102
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:03,620
where it's going, when to do reassembly,

103
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:06,360
when to do all of the functions in a distributed fashion.

104
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:10,300
Kleinrock's work was a dawning light on Licklider's vision.

105
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:15,180
It was the aha that computer communication could one day be a reality.

106
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:18,220
It also marked a revolutionary departure

107
00:08:18,221 --> 00:08:22,140
from the telephone system's use of a technology called circuit switching.

108
00:08:22,141 --> 00:08:26,360
When you make a telephone call, you pick up the phone and you dial a number.

109
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:31,520
And the switching system that connects you with the other telephone

110
00:08:31,521 --> 00:08:36,120
actually creates a circuit between the two machines, between the two telephones.

111
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:39,420
And that connection is dedicated to the conversation.

112
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:43,640
Even if you're not saying anything, the communications path is in place,

113
00:08:43,700 --> 00:08:46,280
and it's kept there until one or the other of you hangs up.

114
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:51,180
The telephone system had an Achilles heel, and still does.

115
00:08:51,181 --> 00:08:55,780
If the connection path between telephones is broken, the call is lost.

116
00:08:56,920 --> 00:09:01,180
That weakness became a matter of national security to the military in the early 60s,

117
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,100
when they feared a Soviet nuclear attack.

118
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:17,820
Besides leveling American cities, the telephone system would be so damaged

119
00:09:17,821 --> 00:09:20,940
that the military would be unable to launch a counterattack.

120
00:09:21,180 --> 00:09:26,680
To assure retaliation, America needed an indestructible communications network.

121
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:30,380
Paul Boran was hired to see if one could be built.

122
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:37,520
We started off by playing with fishnet-type networks,

123
00:09:38,020 --> 00:09:41,720
so that if you chop it up, there's still a path through the network.

124
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:44,100
Now, the problem was we didn't know how to do that.

125
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:50,940
So we came up with the notion of what we called the hot potato rooting,

126
00:09:51,180 --> 00:09:56,400
Sort of a demonstration that it's doable so the traffic can find its way through the network.

127
00:09:57,020 --> 00:09:59,620
But to do that, you've got to chop the data up in little pieces.

128
00:10:00,260 --> 00:10:04,440
Hot potato routing meant a message transiting a network got to where it was going,

129
00:10:04,660 --> 00:10:06,720
even if part of the network was destroyed.

130
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:08,400
But how?

131
00:10:08,820 --> 00:10:12,740
Buran was inspired by a previous study of mice in a maze.

132
00:10:12,741 --> 00:10:21,740
Claude Shannon, in 1952, built a machine that modeled a mouse finding its way through a maze.

133
00:10:21,741 --> 00:10:29,740
And he said, well, if we can model a mouse going through a maze, then we should be able to do the same with information.

134
00:10:29,741 --> 00:10:38,740
So the information flows along in each of the packets that's necessary to find its way through the network.

135
00:10:38,741 --> 00:10:40,740
That's the key.

136
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:45,540
Next, borrowing an idea from the telegraph,

137
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:52,060
Buran decided to chop up each message into small, equal-sized pieces, called packets.

138
00:10:52,820 --> 00:10:56,920
Each packet was like a postcard, with a to and a from address.

139
00:10:58,040 --> 00:11:02,360
Each packet gets a header put on it, which says where that packet wants to go,

140
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:06,860
and you launch that packet into the network, and it goes hop, hop, hop through the network,

141
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:10,720
followed on the heels by the other packets, and they may take different paths.

142
00:11:10,721 --> 00:11:15,180
But no dedicated path is constantly available for them.

143
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:17,920
They'll find their way along paths that are available.

144
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:25,460
Then, Baran allowed each node to decide where to send each packet as it traversed the network.

145
00:11:26,480 --> 00:11:32,720
A node was like a post office that knows where next to send a postcard as it continues its way from sender to recipient.

146
00:11:32,721 --> 00:11:37,260
But unlike a post office, a node would keep copies of the packets

147
00:11:37,261 --> 00:11:41,900
and keep sending them out until they arrive successfully at the next node.

148
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:45,700
This design for a network, a packet switching network,

149
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:50,620
gets its messages through better than telephones as long as some part of the network functions.

150
00:11:52,040 --> 00:11:57,180
The telephone system, which relies on dedicated circuits, fails when circuits are broken.

151
00:11:57,181 --> 00:12:04,180
By 1962, there was enough research and theory to show that a viable computer network could be built.

152
00:12:04,181 --> 00:12:12,180
But implementing one had to wait seven years, until 1969, coincidentally the same year man first walked on the moon.

153
00:12:12,181 --> 00:12:18,180
While this space triumph grew out of a national fear of Soviet domination,

154
00:12:18,181 --> 00:12:25,180
the first computer network stemmed from the frustrations of a single ARPA scientist, Bob Taylor.

155
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:32,580
In the early 1960s, surfing was the search for the perfect wave.

156
00:12:33,540 --> 00:12:35,800
Mail was only delivered by the post office.

157
00:12:36,680 --> 00:12:39,420
Online meant waiting behind someone at the movie theater.

158
00:12:39,980 --> 00:12:42,800
And the server was a waitress at your favorite drive-in.

159
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:44,520
Hello, can I take your order?

160
00:12:44,680 --> 00:12:46,100
Make mine a ham sandwich and coffee.

161
00:12:47,320 --> 00:12:48,300
Mail, mail, mail!

162
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:53,920
But as the decade wore on, new ideas were reshaping America.

163
00:12:54,680 --> 00:12:56,680
For some, civil rights was the cause.

164
00:12:56,681 --> 00:12:58,560
or stopping the Vietnam War.

165
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:01,200
For others, it was landing on the moon.

166
00:13:01,201 --> 00:13:02,360
Pitch 212.

167
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:03,480
Okay, engine stop.

168
00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:04,760
We copy you down, Eagle.

169
00:13:05,300 --> 00:13:07,340
Listen, Tranquility Base here.

170
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:08,540
The Eagle has landed.

171
00:13:09,020 --> 00:13:11,300
Roger, you got a bunch of guys about to turn blue.

172
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:12,580
But in the world of computers,

173
00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:16,400
the new big idea was something called time-sharing.

174
00:13:17,340 --> 00:13:19,920
The old computers had been big machines

175
00:13:19,921 --> 00:13:21,200
that you punched cards

176
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:23,600
and you submitted the cards in decks

177
00:13:23,601 --> 00:13:25,260
and the next day you got the answers.

178
00:13:25,261 --> 00:13:33,679
But in 1969, timesharing was happening, where you would actually sit at a terminal and you would type in your questions and your programs, and the answer would come

179
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:34,860
back two seconds later.

180
00:13:35,540 --> 00:13:43,080
If you were very avant-garde, you had a terminal in your house, and you were logging into a computer across town.

181
00:13:43,081 --> 00:13:50,840
In 1966, Bob Taylor of ARPA, the Advanced Research Projects Agency, needed a separate

182
00:13:50,841 --> 00:13:55,840
terminal to log into each of several timeshared computers at research centers across the country.

183
00:13:56,840 --> 00:14:02,260
No two computers could talk to each other, so Taylor had to move from terminal to terminal

184
00:14:02,261 --> 00:14:04,477
to work on a different computer.

185
00:14:04,478 --> 00:14:08,980
He knew a single terminal could suffice if all the computers were

186
00:14:08,981 --> 00:14:10,180
in a common network.

187
00:14:12,020 --> 00:14:14,640
Taylor wanted to build a network

188
00:14:14,641 --> 00:14:16,100
because he thought it was crazy

189
00:14:16,101 --> 00:14:17,800
that he had to have three different terminals

190
00:14:17,801 --> 00:14:19,840
to talk to three different machines.

191
00:14:20,100 --> 00:14:21,080
Instead of one terminal,

192
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:22,180
they could talk to all three.

193
00:14:22,440 --> 00:14:24,320
So his notion was to pursue

194
00:14:24,321 --> 00:14:25,900
the creation of a network

195
00:14:25,901 --> 00:14:28,980
which would link heterogeneous computers

196
00:14:28,981 --> 00:14:31,980
to each other through this network.

197
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:34,360
Taylor asked his boss for money

198
00:14:34,361 --> 00:14:35,980
to build a computer network,

199
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:38,400
arguing that it could save ARPA time,

200
00:14:38,401 --> 00:14:39,880
money, and resources.

201
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:48,620
It was a very straightforward connect researchers to expensive computers that you would not want to replicate.

202
00:14:49,720 --> 00:14:51,220
Resource sharing was the term.

203
00:14:51,480 --> 00:14:56,100
It was logging into remote computers, but then it very quickly went from logging into remote computers

204
00:14:56,101 --> 00:14:58,900
to transferring files among these expensive computers.

205
00:15:00,680 --> 00:15:05,180
What Taylor wanted had neither the hype nor the expense of going to the moon.

206
00:15:05,181 --> 00:15:13,180
He was allocated one million dollars to turn the theories of a computer network into a reality.

207
00:15:13,181 --> 00:15:18,180
This was enough to start a chain reaction that changed the world.

208
00:15:18,181 --> 00:15:22,180
Of course, at the time, nobody knew it.

209
00:15:22,181 --> 00:15:26,180
We all knew that we were dealing with very powerful technology.

210
00:15:26,181 --> 00:15:32,180
But I don't think any of us 20 years ago or 25 years ago had any appreciation for what it would be like

211
00:15:32,181 --> 00:15:37,840
like if, you know, everybody on the planet had access to information or could produce

212
00:15:37,841 --> 00:15:39,780
information and share it.

213
00:15:39,781 --> 00:15:42,960
Taylor recruited Larry Roberts for the job.

214
00:15:42,961 --> 00:15:48,320
Roberts had built the world's first experimental connection between two computers at the MIT

215
00:15:48,321 --> 00:15:51,300
Lincoln Laboratories in Lexington, Massachusetts.

216
00:15:51,301 --> 00:15:57,740
I knew that I knew how to do it because I actually had built the first network.

217
00:15:57,741 --> 00:16:02,060
I had lived with Len Kleinrock at MIT and learned about packet switching and queuing,

218
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:03,660
because he was my office mate.

219
00:16:04,120 --> 00:16:07,280
And so I had built the connection between the two computers

220
00:16:07,281 --> 00:16:10,160
as basically the same kind of connection that OpenNet had,

221
00:16:10,161 --> 00:16:12,420
except that now we were going to distribute it to more people.

222
00:16:14,400 --> 00:16:18,280
Roberts wasn't sure about the best way to network mainframe computers.

223
00:16:18,920 --> 00:16:20,820
One way would be to link them in a chain.

224
00:16:21,420 --> 00:16:23,040
But this design had a flaw.

225
00:16:23,041 --> 00:16:30,040
If you tried to interconnect the large computers directly, then each of those large computers

226
00:16:30,041 --> 00:16:34,040
would have to figure out how to work with each of the other large computers.

227
00:16:34,041 --> 00:16:39,040
So they would have a tremendous problem in their software to be able to figure out the

228
00:16:39,041 --> 00:16:44,040
rules and procedures and protocols for dealing with ten different kinds of computers out

229
00:16:44,041 --> 00:16:45,040
in the country.

230
00:16:45,041 --> 00:16:50,040
On a cab ride home from a conference, Roberts was inspired with a better way to connect

231
00:16:50,041 --> 00:16:55,220
to connect the computers when a fellow engineer suggested building identical special-purpose

232
00:16:55,221 --> 00:17:00,360
mini-computers to form the network and tie each timeshared computer to one of them.

233
00:17:01,340 --> 00:17:05,500
This way they only had to learn how to deal with the one little computer that you put there.

234
00:17:06,940 --> 00:17:12,580
It was a brilliant simplification, and it made the universities more willing to participate

235
00:17:12,581 --> 00:17:16,480
in the network because it would not interfere with their own computer projects.

236
00:17:16,481 --> 00:17:21,180
On paper, Roberts had the network design figured out.

237
00:17:21,780 --> 00:17:24,480
Now all he had to do was find someone to build it.

238
00:17:24,920 --> 00:17:26,680
But it wouldn't be the telephone company.

239
00:17:27,540 --> 00:17:31,200
The phone companies, A, believed it couldn't be done.

240
00:17:31,780 --> 00:17:35,440
B, didn't think it would be useful or anybody would want it if it were done.

241
00:17:35,580 --> 00:17:40,240
And C, had huge investments in their current communication systems and weren't interested.

242
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:45,540
If there was going to be a computer network, someone else had to build it.

243
00:17:45,541 --> 00:17:49,580
But who?

244
00:17:49,581 --> 00:17:56,580
By the standards of today, 1968 was a medieval time of communication technology.

245
00:17:56,581 --> 00:18:00,580
Typewriters, not word processors, pounded out letters.

246
00:18:00,581 --> 00:18:03,580
Most telephones used rotary dials.

247
00:18:03,581 --> 00:18:06,940
Black and white television was as common as color.

248
00:18:06,941 --> 00:18:11,540
The height of high tech was the lunar module, which had less computing power than today's

249
00:18:11,541 --> 00:18:14,540
personal computer.

250
00:18:14,541 --> 00:18:25,136
1968 also was the year Larry Roberts sought out the engineers to build the Advanced Research Projects Agency's computer network, the ARPANET, the acorn that would grow

251
00:18:25,137 --> 00:18:27,040
to become the mighty Internet.

252
00:18:27,960 --> 00:18:38,000
It was going to be based on special purpose mini computers called interface message processors, or IMPs, and a revolutionary technology called packet switching.

253
00:18:38,480 --> 00:18:41,844
So here's the way it works. Suppose I want to send a long document.

254
00:18:41,845 --> 00:18:43,200
I feed it into the network.

255
00:18:43,201 --> 00:18:47,200
The first thing the network does is chop that up into a lot of little pieces called packets.

256
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:53,260
And each packet is launched into the network, and it tries to find its way to its destination,

257
00:18:53,560 --> 00:18:55,340
each packet operating independently.

258
00:18:56,400 --> 00:18:59,980
At the other end, the final destination takes all these packets,

259
00:19:00,140 --> 00:19:02,680
think of those little postcards carrying your data,

260
00:19:03,060 --> 00:19:07,520
puts them back into a long document, and delivers them to the recipient at the other end.

261
00:19:08,180 --> 00:19:11,260
The plan was to network four identical imps.

262
00:19:11,261 --> 00:19:23,360
Each one was to be attached to a different kind of mainframe computer, a Sigma-7 at UCLA, an SDS-940 at the Stanford Research Institute, an IBM 360-75 at the

263
00:19:23,361 --> 00:19:29,680
University of California at Santa Barbara, and a PDP-10 at the University of Utah.

264
00:19:29,681 --> 00:19:36,680
Roberts asked 140 companies, including AT&T and IBM, to bid on the project.

265
00:19:36,681 --> 00:19:40,680
But the giants of communication and computers refused.

266
00:19:40,681 --> 00:19:42,680
They said it wouldn't work.

267
00:19:42,681 --> 00:19:44,680
Well, they didn't think it would work.

268
00:19:44,681 --> 00:19:47,680
They didn't see that it was very important or mainstream.

269
00:19:47,681 --> 00:19:51,680
I mean, after all, communications, we've got our own communication systems here at IBM,

270
00:19:51,681 --> 00:19:56,680
and AT&T does the telephone system. What else do we need? What's your problem?

271
00:19:56,681 --> 00:20:00,500
So the telecom guys would say, little boy, go away.

272
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:02,540
You represent no revenue to us.

273
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:04,080
And they were right, we didn't.

274
00:20:04,620 --> 00:20:08,080
So little boy went away and created Packet Switching on its own.

275
00:20:09,440 --> 00:20:16,020
A small Cambridge, Massachusetts company, Bolt, Brannock & Newman, known as BBN, wasn't sure either.

276
00:20:16,420 --> 00:20:17,700
But they were willing to try.

277
00:20:18,580 --> 00:20:20,540
Frank Hart headed the BBN team.

278
00:20:21,200 --> 00:20:23,860
Well, we had to, first of all, figure out if we knew how to do it.

279
00:20:23,861 --> 00:20:30,740
I think it was a relatively revolutionary concept to put together a packet switching communication system,

280
00:20:31,240 --> 00:20:32,700
and no one had done that before.

281
00:20:34,300 --> 00:20:40,240
BBN won the contract and went to work building the emps for the ARPANET on January 1, 1969.

282
00:20:41,240 --> 00:20:43,740
The first was due at UCLA in nine months.

283
00:20:44,580 --> 00:20:48,320
Hart's team faced a crush of obstacles and very little time.

284
00:20:48,321 --> 00:20:54,560
It's not like a scientific discovery of the second law of thermodynamics or finding what

285
00:20:54,561 --> 00:20:56,280
DNA molecules look like.

286
00:20:56,281 --> 00:21:03,260
It was an engineering problem with dozens of very difficult, messy, undone engineering

287
00:21:03,261 --> 00:21:04,480
problems.

288
00:21:04,481 --> 00:21:08,260
And some of them were simply the fact that it was such a rushed schedule.

289
00:21:08,261 --> 00:21:14,340
UCLA was also under a deadline to write the software so its Sigma-7 computer could communicate

290
00:21:14,341 --> 00:21:15,600
with the imp.

291
00:21:15,601 --> 00:21:22,100
Ironically, graduate students were chosen to forge this first important link in the world's first computer network.

292
00:21:23,180 --> 00:21:27,880
The senior professors who were in charge of each of the projects, the principal investigators, as they're called,

293
00:21:28,440 --> 00:21:33,260
really did not spend much time thinking about or focusing on it.

294
00:21:33,261 --> 00:21:39,140
It was left to the next tier down, to the graduate students or, in some cases, staff members at places like SRI.

295
00:21:40,900 --> 00:21:43,480
Just figure out something useful to do with this.

296
00:21:43,481 --> 00:21:50,480
Crocker wrote the code to join UCLA's computer to the first imp in what was to become the ARPANET.

297
00:21:50,481 --> 00:22:01,480
The idea of having one computer send a program over to another computer was a big leap in some ways, but very natural in other ways.

298
00:22:01,481 --> 00:22:04,480
But in those days there was no commonality across the computers,

299
00:22:04,481 --> 00:22:09,480
and so part of what we had to think about was in order to make that idea work there would have to be a common language,

300
00:22:09,481 --> 00:22:13,840
would have to be a common language, and we actually spent time trying to define what

301
00:22:13,841 --> 00:22:15,240
that language might be.

302
00:22:17,140 --> 00:22:21,560
Back at BBN, Hart's team was facing numerous engineering challenges.

303
00:22:22,500 --> 00:22:25,900
One was, how do you stop the packets from circulating forever?

304
00:22:26,660 --> 00:22:29,200
How do they know when to stop at their destination?

305
00:22:30,580 --> 00:22:35,900
You dump a piece of message into the system, and now it's to wind its way through it, at

306
00:22:35,901 --> 00:22:37,940
each node picking the best path.

307
00:22:37,941 --> 00:22:40,240
Well, what stops it from circulating forever?

308
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:43,240
That was a very difficult technical problem.

309
00:22:43,320 --> 00:22:44,240
No one had done that.

310
00:22:45,060 --> 00:22:47,740
Another thing, a host has a big message to send.

311
00:22:47,820 --> 00:22:49,120
It dumps a big message in.

312
00:22:49,380 --> 00:22:50,940
What stops it from overflowing?

313
00:22:51,120 --> 00:22:55,140
What protects the system from just having this stuff fall on the floor?

314
00:22:56,060 --> 00:22:58,400
And there was a concern about the phone lines.

315
00:22:58,980 --> 00:23:01,040
They were built for voice traffic, not data.

316
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:04,920
Special hardware and software was required

317
00:23:04,921 --> 00:23:08,600
so that when errors occurred, the imps could retransmit the message.

318
00:23:11,760 --> 00:23:15,840
Also, there was no guarantee the mainframe computers would hold up.

319
00:23:16,420 --> 00:23:20,240
They were just not built to run non-stop as communications machines.

320
00:23:21,120 --> 00:23:25,180
Even worse, how would you know where problems were occurring to fix them?

321
00:23:26,020 --> 00:23:29,120
When some guy in some sight hit a key on his keyboard,

322
00:23:29,660 --> 00:23:32,680
the message had to go from his keyboard to his big computer,

323
00:23:32,820 --> 00:23:34,600
from his big computer to the little computer,

324
00:23:34,920 --> 00:23:37,380
through a whole bunch of other little imps at other sites,

325
00:23:37,480 --> 00:23:39,360
and finally to a one at the destination,

326
00:23:39,740 --> 00:23:42,340
and then into the host, and then they had to come back all the way.

327
00:23:42,600 --> 00:23:44,000
And when the guy didn't get an answer,

328
00:23:44,280 --> 00:23:46,560
he didn't care where it broke, he just didn't like it.

329
00:23:46,640 --> 00:23:48,640
He kicked it, you know, he would yell and scream.

330
00:23:48,980 --> 00:23:51,500
So you had to have a way of finding out where it broke.

331
00:23:51,920 --> 00:23:55,560
And that was, again, a difficult problem for the network authority,

332
00:23:55,561 --> 00:23:57,060
which was us in this case.

333
00:23:58,880 --> 00:24:03,260
There were glitches up until the day BBN shipped the first imp to UCLA.

334
00:24:03,261 --> 00:24:07,260
In fact, BBN could have kept on improving it forever.

335
00:24:07,261 --> 00:24:10,260
There were people in the group that wanted it perfect,

336
00:24:10,261 --> 00:24:11,260
and there were other people that said,

337
00:24:11,261 --> 00:24:13,260
come on, come on, we've got to get it out of here in nine months.

338
00:24:13,261 --> 00:24:15,260
It has to be just good enough.

339
00:24:15,261 --> 00:24:18,260
That was the kind of decision that I ended up having to make.

340
00:24:18,261 --> 00:24:22,260
What was good enough, and where did one seek more perfection?

341
00:24:24,260 --> 00:24:30,260
On September 1, 1969, the first piece of hardware created for the computer network

342
00:24:30,261 --> 00:24:35,260
network that was the forerunner of the Internet arrived at UCLA.

343
00:24:35,261 --> 00:24:40,080
What I do remember is that they slid the thing in place, turned the power on, and the lights

344
00:24:40,081 --> 00:24:45,680
immediately started blinking because the program had been put in before it left BB&N and it

345
00:24:45,681 --> 00:24:47,040
was just turned off.

346
00:24:47,041 --> 00:24:50,080
When they turned it on again, it picked up right where it left off.

347
00:24:50,081 --> 00:24:52,100
Now there wasn't much of a network to speak of.

348
00:24:52,101 --> 00:24:53,420
There was the first note at UCLA.

349
00:24:53,421 --> 00:24:58,500
They didn't have anybody to talk to, so we just sent traffic to ourselves.

350
00:24:58,501 --> 00:25:03,680
By the end of 1969, all four sites were interconnected.

351
00:25:03,681 --> 00:25:08,060
Had this primitive computer network failed, the effort to network computers might have

352
00:25:08,061 --> 00:25:13,320
been scrapped as just another project beyond the reach of current technology.

353
00:25:13,321 --> 00:25:15,500
But it worked.

354
00:25:15,501 --> 00:25:22,500
In April 1971, the ARPANET had 18 mainframe computers hooked into the network.

355
00:25:22,501 --> 00:25:28,020
Bob Metcalf was a grad student who connected MIT's computer to it.

356
00:25:28,021 --> 00:25:29,660
The thrill was connecting things together.

357
00:25:29,780 --> 00:25:31,180
You know, there's action at a distance.

358
00:25:31,400 --> 00:25:34,960
You know, you can wiggle something here and it moves over there.

359
00:25:35,460 --> 00:25:40,820
So I was able to form packets of bits and send them.

360
00:25:41,240 --> 00:25:44,960
And over the phone, the guy in California would say, I got it.

361
00:25:45,660 --> 00:25:47,000
And I'd say, well, send it back.

362
00:25:47,100 --> 00:25:48,180
And then he'd send it back.

363
00:25:48,181 --> 00:25:54,360
It was the thrill of reaching out and touching someone or something far away.

364
00:25:54,361 --> 00:26:00,280
At first, there wasn't much to do on the ARPANET beyond transferring data files among its members.

365
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,220
But then, a new capability was added.

366
00:26:03,800 --> 00:26:06,860
The first killer application that would bring in the masses

367
00:26:06,861 --> 00:26:10,420
and take the usefulness of computer networking to a new level.

368
00:26:10,920 --> 00:26:13,020
That killer app was email.

369
00:26:14,500 --> 00:26:19,640
I was actually working on a mail program, but it was a program for a single computer

370
00:26:19,641 --> 00:26:24,840
so that you could send messages from yourself to another user on the same time-sharing computer.

371
00:26:25,580 --> 00:26:28,800
And I had written a couple of programs to transfer files,

372
00:26:29,100 --> 00:26:34,400
and it occurred to me that since the mailbox that I was sending these messages to

373
00:26:34,401 --> 00:26:38,580
was nothing more than a file, I could send that file across the network

374
00:26:38,581 --> 00:26:41,640
instead of keeping it within the same machine.

375
00:26:42,280 --> 00:26:46,080
And so I put two programs together, modified the code a little bit,

376
00:26:46,081 --> 00:26:49,880
and the first email program was created.

377
00:26:50,900 --> 00:26:53,920
Tomlinson did more than just write the code for email.

378
00:26:54,700 --> 00:27:00,480
In a keystroke, he gave the world a new icon for the information age, the at sign.

379
00:27:01,080 --> 00:27:04,380
The at sign was the most obvious choice

380
00:27:04,381 --> 00:27:07,940
because we're talking about a user who was at some computer.

381
00:27:08,740 --> 00:27:09,500
It just made sense.

382
00:27:10,420 --> 00:27:14,520
Ironically, what would become one of the most popular applications on the Internet

383
00:27:14,521 --> 00:27:17,700
was a program Tomlinson wrote on his own time.

384
00:27:18,240 --> 00:27:19,740
It seemed like a neat idea.

385
00:27:20,940 --> 00:27:23,400
Nobody was clamoring for email.

386
00:27:23,620 --> 00:27:28,120
Our sponsor, Department of Defense, didn't say anything about wanting email.

387
00:27:28,780 --> 00:27:30,260
My boss didn't say anything about email.

388
00:27:31,760 --> 00:27:33,820
But, in fact, nobody said anything about email

389
00:27:33,821 --> 00:27:36,720
because the term wasn't coined until several years later.

390
00:27:36,940 --> 00:27:41,180
But it just seemed like an interesting thing to do with a computer and a network.

391
00:27:41,740 --> 00:27:42,620
And so I just did it.

392
00:27:42,621 --> 00:27:49,620
So then electronic mail became the completely unanticipated killer app, as we say, for the ARPANET.

393
00:27:49,621 --> 00:27:55,620
We went from remote login, file transfer, electronic mail in a year flat or two years flat,

394
00:27:55,621 --> 00:27:59,620
and then it stayed that way for 20 years.

395
00:27:59,621 --> 00:28:05,620
In October 1972, ARPA turned science fiction into science fact

396
00:28:05,621 --> 00:28:09,620
when it demonstrated the computer network in Washington, D.C.

397
00:28:09,621 --> 00:28:21,620
But unlike flying saucers, this idea really did take over the world.

398
00:28:21,621 --> 00:28:27,220
The telephone, television, radio and postal systems are essential communication infrastructures

399
00:28:27,221 --> 00:28:31,520
for life as we know it.

400
00:28:31,521 --> 00:28:37,180
But as the world enters a new millennium, it appears as if the Internet, begun in 1969

401
00:28:37,181 --> 00:28:42,980
an experimental computer network, is gradually absorbing many of their functions.

402
00:28:42,981 --> 00:28:47,380
The Internet can do that because it was built with an open architecture.

403
00:28:47,381 --> 00:28:52,920
It's a super highway with plenty of on-ramps, lanes and no traffic restrictions.

404
00:28:52,921 --> 00:28:55,820
It handles whatever comes down the road.

405
00:28:55,821 --> 00:29:01,280
This generality of purpose is the brilliance of its design.

406
00:29:01,281 --> 00:29:06,520
We were very conscious that we were opening up new territory and that rather than pick

407
00:29:06,521 --> 00:29:13,440
very limited applications that we ought to lay a foundation that was as broad and open-ended as possible.

408
00:29:14,640 --> 00:29:16,700
Inclusion guided the network's development.

409
00:29:17,560 --> 00:29:21,040
Anyone could contribute ideas about its use and how to improve it.

410
00:29:21,960 --> 00:29:27,620
Ideas were offered as requests for comments so everyone would feel free to discuss and criticize them.

411
00:29:27,621 --> 00:29:37,860
I wanted to emphasize the point that we were not making assertions or edicts, but that

412
00:29:37,861 --> 00:29:42,900
we were trying to create a dialogue, that we were putting forth ideas and that it was

413
00:29:42,901 --> 00:29:45,720
supposed to engender responses.

414
00:29:45,721 --> 00:29:48,500
And so we made the simplest possible rules.

415
00:29:48,501 --> 00:29:53,680
Anybody could write anything they want, that it had no status, that it got whatever credibility

416
00:29:53,681 --> 00:29:57,020
it got from its content, not from the fact that it had been blessed by somebody.

417
00:29:57,021 --> 00:30:02,020
There's a kind of Internet culture which grew out of the academic community.

418
00:30:02,021 --> 00:30:06,020
What you find is that there's a tradition of sharing of knowledge

419
00:30:06,021 --> 00:30:08,020
and people will add to it.

420
00:30:08,021 --> 00:30:11,020
They will find mistakes and bugs and they'll fix them.

421
00:30:11,021 --> 00:30:13,020
They'll tell you about them and everybody else.

422
00:30:13,021 --> 00:30:18,020
The rate of evolution, the rate at which the new functionality can be added

423
00:30:18,021 --> 00:30:23,020
is very higher, much higher, simply because so many people are contributing to it.

424
00:30:23,021 --> 00:30:28,020
By 1972, the ARPANET had fewer than 25 sites online.

425
00:30:28,640 --> 00:30:29,680
Yet more were coming.

426
00:30:30,680 --> 00:30:35,260
What sparked the increase was the first international conference on computers and communication,

427
00:30:35,800 --> 00:30:38,740
sponsored by ARPA as a coming-out party for its network.

428
00:30:40,620 --> 00:30:47,280
Bob Metcalf, fresh from MIT and Harvard, was in charge of compiling a list of just what the network could do.

429
00:30:47,281 --> 00:30:53,120
So all I did was contact all the people who had computers on the ARPANET,

430
00:30:53,160 --> 00:30:57,040
and there weren't very many, like 19 or 23 or some number,

431
00:30:57,100 --> 00:31:00,080
and said, do you have something interesting that's going on at your host

432
00:31:00,081 --> 00:31:02,420
that you'd like other people to access over the net?

433
00:31:03,280 --> 00:31:07,420
Metcalfe could only find a few esoteric uses for the fledgling network,

434
00:31:07,900 --> 00:31:11,960
but at this early stage, that was enough for an impressive demonstration.

435
00:31:11,961 --> 00:31:21,200
By the time of this demo, I could find 19, 19, imagine that, 19 different things that you could do on the net.

436
00:31:21,840 --> 00:31:24,260
And they had a program that pretended to be a psychotic.

437
00:31:24,580 --> 00:31:26,220
They had games that you could play.

438
00:31:26,400 --> 00:31:31,200
They had demonstrations of symbolic manipulation of mathematical formulas.

439
00:31:31,560 --> 00:31:38,900
They had more technical things like remote job entry for submitting jobs from one computer to the next and getting the results back.

440
00:31:38,901 --> 00:31:43,900
But demonstrations sometimes go wrong, and at the wrong time.

441
00:31:43,901 --> 00:31:47,900
Well, imagine a graduate student, big red beard.

442
00:31:47,901 --> 00:31:53,900
I put together this book, and in walks ten AT&T executives, all in pinstripe suits.

443
00:31:53,901 --> 00:32:00,900
And they needed someone to give them a tour of the ARPANET, so I was the natural choice, having done the book.

444
00:32:00,901 --> 00:32:04,900
So they gave me these ten executives, and I started giving them the tour.

445
00:32:04,901 --> 00:32:08,740
them the tour and right in the middle of the tour the ARPANET for the only time

446
00:32:08,741 --> 00:32:12,160
during the two or three days of the demonstration the net crashed the

447
00:32:12,161 --> 00:32:19,120
computer the imp crashed and the demo froze and here's this poor graduate

448
00:32:19,121 --> 00:32:22,480
student having worked on this for several years and being his life's work

449
00:32:22,481 --> 00:32:27,760
and he turns around and these ten AT&T executives thought it was funny in fact

450
00:32:27,761 --> 00:32:32,020
were somewhat amused and happy that it had crashed because this was very

451
00:32:32,021 --> 00:32:38,440
reassuring to their lifestyle and their technology that this packet switching was just too

452
00:32:38,441 --> 00:32:40,280
unreliable and they didn't have to worry about it.

453
00:32:40,281 --> 00:32:46,420
And the fact that they were amused angered me greatly, angered me.

454
00:32:46,500 --> 00:32:50,420
In fact, to this day, many years later, I still have a thing against telephone companies

455
00:32:50,421 --> 00:32:51,121
for that reason.

456
00:32:52,900 --> 00:32:56,860
After the conference, the development of computer networks took off.

457
00:32:57,720 --> 00:33:00,920
Networks soon began to multiply throughout the Western world.

458
00:33:02,020 --> 00:33:08,020
The National Science Foundation made being on their network a requisite for receiving grant money.

459
00:33:09,280 --> 00:33:10,860
Soon other networks emerged.

460
00:33:11,340 --> 00:33:15,340
There were LANs, or local area networks, to link computers throughout offices,

461
00:33:16,260 --> 00:33:21,620
and WANs, or wide area networks, to link computers across buildings or campuses.

462
00:33:22,460 --> 00:33:26,640
But each new network was like a foreign country speaking its own language.

463
00:33:27,020 --> 00:33:31,140
Could disparate networks be interconnected to make an Internet work?

464
00:33:32,020 --> 00:33:41,020
In 1973, Ben Cerf and Bob Kahn teamed up to develop a set of rules or procedures known as the TCP IP protocol

465
00:33:41,021 --> 00:33:47,020
that, if followed by different computer networks, would allow them to pass their message packets back and forth.

466
00:33:48,020 --> 00:33:57,020
The protocolon, which actually is the term used to describe a little piece of papyrus that goes on the front of a scroll.

467
00:33:57,021 --> 00:33:57,721
scroll.

468
00:33:57,722 --> 00:34:01,520
But the protocol on this is in fact the little thing that tells you what's in

469
00:34:01,521 --> 00:34:03,272
the rest of the scroll.

470
00:34:03,273 --> 00:34:08,300
We use that term because packets have headers on them at the front

471
00:34:08,301 --> 00:34:12,080
that say where they're going, where they came from, and what information they contain, and

472
00:34:12,081 --> 00:34:16,300
then comes the information itself. We thought that represented a protocol too.

473
00:34:16,301 --> 00:34:19,999
The TCP IP protocol enabled the networks to interconnect.

474
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:22,140
Cerf and Kahn called the computer

475
00:34:22,141 --> 00:34:26,680
in the network that would know the protocol and thus be able to communicate across networks

476
00:34:26,681 --> 00:34:27,680
the gateway.

477
00:34:27,681 --> 00:34:32,560
This puzzle has some pieces already on the board.

478
00:34:32,561 --> 00:34:34,560
I had computers of various kinds.

479
00:34:34,561 --> 00:34:36,840
I had networks of various kinds.

480
00:34:36,841 --> 00:34:39,460
And I wasn't allowed to mess around with the networks.

481
00:34:39,461 --> 00:34:42,560
The only place that could know were the outside of the networks.

482
00:34:42,561 --> 00:34:44,360
Well, what's outside the networks?

483
00:34:44,361 --> 00:34:46,440
The host computers are outside.

484
00:34:46,441 --> 00:34:51,840
And then I needed something that would interconnect the networks to each other.

485
00:34:51,841 --> 00:34:56,660
And as Bob and I discussed all this, we concluded we needed something that we call the gateway.

486
00:34:56,661 --> 00:35:01,260
gateway that knew how to talk to each of the networks it was connected to, which could

487
00:35:01,261 --> 00:35:11,260
be two or three or four, and could encapsulate, envelope, packets coming from the host computers

488
00:35:11,261 --> 00:35:15,480
into packets going through the underlying networks.

489
00:35:16,420 --> 00:35:21,360
Others proposed different protocols, but if there wasn't a common one for all networks

490
00:35:21,361 --> 00:35:27,100
to follow, a common language, there would be several Internets instead of a single unified one.

491
00:35:28,060 --> 00:35:35,640
Finally, in 1983, after 10 years of refinement and bureaucratic haggling, TCPIP was adopted as

492
00:35:35,641 --> 00:35:42,300
the universal standard, marking a milestone in the development of the Internet, a common network of

493
00:35:42,301 --> 00:35:44,519
interconnected networks.

494
00:35:44,520 --> 00:35:50,900
If we have enough in common, if we hold enough in common, then we can

495
00:35:50,901 --> 00:35:56,015
communicate. If we don't hold enough in common, it's much harder.

496
00:35:56,016 --> 00:35:58,140
So in fact, the reason that

497
00:35:58,141 --> 00:36:04,540
the network works at all is that we have worked very hard to create all this commonality so that

498
00:36:04,541 --> 00:36:10,483
we share enough knowledge to make it possible to convey information to each other.

499
00:36:10,484 --> 00:36:11,280
The ARPANET

500
00:36:11,281 --> 00:36:16,420
was developed as a tool for defense research and restricted to select universities and think tanks

501
00:36:16,421 --> 00:36:20,558
engaged in government projects, not individuals or businesses.

502
00:36:20,559 --> 00:36:22,760
For this reason, private networks

503
00:36:22,761 --> 00:36:28,000
like Prodigy, CompuServe, and what came to be called America Online started to spring

504
00:36:28,001 --> 00:36:33,300
up where individuals could join in. Still, the Internet had not reached Main Street,

505
00:36:33,440 --> 00:36:39,011
USA, but a major milestone in its young life was about to see all that change.

506
00:36:39,012 --> 00:36:40,440
Unlike technological

507
00:36:40,441 --> 00:36:45,180
breakthroughs that swell the use of computer networks, this milestone was political.

508
00:36:46,420 --> 00:36:50,520
It would unleash the full power of free enterprise and American ingenuity

509
00:36:50,521 --> 00:36:55,340
and bring a worldwide communications network into the home and office.

510
00:37:00,360 --> 00:37:04,120
The Internet grew by the fate and fortune of new technologies.

511
00:37:05,480 --> 00:37:09,860
Among them were breakthroughs in electronics that brought mainframe computer power

512
00:37:09,861 --> 00:37:12,720
down to the size and cost of a desktop PC.

513
00:37:12,721 --> 00:37:19,680
The silicon chip, the high-speed modem, and the computer mouse are as significant in shaping

514
00:37:19,681 --> 00:37:22,600
the use of the Internet as the first imp.

515
00:37:22,601 --> 00:37:28,820
However, one of the Internet's major milestones was achieved not by technology, but by an

516
00:37:28,821 --> 00:37:31,740
act of Congress.

517
00:37:31,741 --> 00:37:37,220
On June 9, 1992, Congress passed a bill taking the Internet out of the exclusive hands of

518
00:37:37,221 --> 00:37:39,440
government and into the public.

519
00:37:39,441 --> 00:37:42,360
The following November, President Bush signed it into law.

520
00:37:42,361 --> 00:37:44,880
The Internet had crossed the Rubicon.

521
00:37:44,881 --> 00:37:48,900
The government, in particular National Science Foundation, which had been investing in research

522
00:37:48,901 --> 00:37:54,120
in the ARPANET and then the Internet, began to get the idea that maybe this should be

523
00:37:54,121 --> 00:37:55,120
turned loose.

524
00:37:55,121 --> 00:38:01,200
This technology should be turned loose into the open world for commercial use, for whatever

525
00:38:01,201 --> 00:38:02,200
use.

526
00:38:02,201 --> 00:38:07,200
And that unleashed a lot of commercial energy and competition and progress.

527
00:38:09,440 --> 00:38:13,940
By allowing free enterprise and individuals access to the Internet,

528
00:38:15,060 --> 00:38:20,000
the seeds were planted that would flourish into many new and previously unimagined uses.

529
00:38:25,480 --> 00:38:31,940
But the dream of easily sharing information among computers was thwarted by the complexity of finding it.

530
00:38:32,360 --> 00:38:35,540
It was like having the Library of Congress at your disposal,

531
00:38:35,541 --> 00:38:41,980
but being unable to gather what was written on a desired topic without tedious and often discouraging hunts.

532
00:38:43,120 --> 00:38:48,920
Under 1980s technology, it was difficult to research a topic without being a computer wizard.

533
00:38:50,820 --> 00:38:54,900
Around the same time that Congress was opening the Internet to more people,

534
00:38:55,460 --> 00:39:01,840
a physicist named Tim Berners-Lee at the CERN Research Institute in Switzerland created an easier way.

535
00:39:01,841 --> 00:39:08,480
CERN was a microcosm of the rest of the world in that it was global.

536
00:39:08,920 --> 00:39:13,020
A lot of people reporting to different institutes, trying to solve problems together.

537
00:39:14,000 --> 00:39:18,380
But unlike the rest of the world, most people had workstations on their desks.

538
00:39:19,020 --> 00:39:23,160
Berners-Lee developed the software to more easily follow threads of knowledge

539
00:39:23,161 --> 00:39:25,740
within the information stored throughout the Internet.

540
00:39:25,741 --> 00:39:31,740
Computers are good at storing information in trees, in matrices.

541
00:39:31,741 --> 00:39:33,740
They'd been programmed to work like that.

542
00:39:33,741 --> 00:39:38,740
They hadn't really been programmed to store information in random associations,

543
00:39:38,741 --> 00:39:42,740
so that when you smell a particular type of coffee

544
00:39:42,741 --> 00:39:44,740
and it takes you back to the coffee house where you first smelt it,

545
00:39:44,741 --> 00:39:46,740
that is the sort of thing a brain can store.

546
00:39:46,741 --> 00:39:49,740
That's not the sort of thing that computers were programmed to store.

547
00:39:49,741 --> 00:39:54,740
By using a computer mouse to click on a word or phrase linked across the Internet,

548
00:39:54,741 --> 00:40:00,740
One could instantaneously follow a link from site to site around the web in pursuit of a topic.

549
00:40:00,741 --> 00:40:05,740
The crisscrossing of these myriad threads across the globe reminded him of a web.

550
00:40:05,741 --> 00:40:10,740
So he named his invention the World Wide Web.

551
00:40:10,741 --> 00:40:13,740
The next thing you know, it started growing on itself.

552
00:40:13,741 --> 00:40:16,740
It went from just Tim in his little computer in Switzerland

553
00:40:16,741 --> 00:40:22,740
to a worldwide phenomenon carrying billions going on trillions of dollars of commerce.

554
00:40:22,741 --> 00:40:24,600
It's just an incredible story.

555
00:40:25,720 --> 00:40:27,580
And completely unexpected.

556
00:40:27,760 --> 00:40:30,060
None of the geniuses who designed the ARPANET in the beginning

557
00:40:30,061 --> 00:40:32,280
had any idea that anything like that would happen.

558
00:40:34,380 --> 00:40:38,420
Even though the World Wide Web made it easier to follow threads of information,

559
00:40:38,780 --> 00:40:41,160
it was not terribly user-friendly at first.

560
00:40:41,640 --> 00:40:44,800
But it gave the Internet a revolutionary new capability

561
00:40:44,801 --> 00:40:47,200
beyond file transfers and email.

562
00:40:47,201 --> 00:40:55,400
Still, in 1992, there were only 50 web pages on the Internet.

563
00:40:55,401 --> 00:41:00,760
If the World Wide Web made the Internet more accessible, another development rocketed it

564
00:41:00,761 --> 00:41:05,240
to near instantaneous acceptance by people far and wide.

565
00:41:05,241 --> 00:41:10,180
This was a very user-friendly software used to browse the World Wide Web.

566
00:41:10,181 --> 00:41:15,700
It was developed in 1993 at the University of Illinois by a 22-year-old student named

567
00:41:15,701 --> 00:41:16,700
Mark Andreessen.

568
00:41:16,701 --> 00:41:22,400
It was called Mosaic and later commercialized by Netscape that enabled access to the World

569
00:41:22,401 --> 00:41:28,400
Wide Web and therefore made it a lot easier for people to get access to content.

570
00:41:28,401 --> 00:41:33,540
Mark Andreessen's browser turned the Internet into a graphically rich world, the very signature

571
00:41:33,541 --> 00:41:35,620
of today's Internet.

572
00:41:35,621 --> 00:41:41,380
It was able to display text, use scroll bars, jump around the Web at the click of a mouse.

573
00:41:41,381 --> 00:41:44,600
In short, it was the essence of user-friendly.

574
00:41:44,601 --> 00:41:48,600
It wasn't the first or the last browser, but it was a breakthrough application.

575
00:41:48,601 --> 00:41:49,600
In this case, a short video like this.

576
00:41:49,601 --> 00:41:54,600
The killer app that brought the fire of the Internet and web to humankind.

577
00:41:54,601 --> 00:42:07,600
In 1993, after Andreessen's browser hit, the web grew by 341,000 percent.

578
00:42:07,601 --> 00:42:12,600
By the late 90s, the Internet had grown in simplicity of use, complexity of performance,

579
00:42:12,601 --> 00:42:14,320
and multiplicity of information.

580
00:42:15,380 --> 00:42:19,360
It now had completely redefined the use of the personal computer.

581
00:42:20,860 --> 00:42:24,480
What was once an in-house word processor and spreadsheet calculator

582
00:42:24,481 --> 00:42:27,620
was by the end of the 20th century

583
00:42:27,621 --> 00:42:30,000
a way to get out of the house without leaving it.

584
00:42:31,080 --> 00:42:33,580
A way to go online with the world.

585
00:42:36,360 --> 00:42:39,500
Well, the only reason you have a television is because of the network

586
00:42:39,501 --> 00:42:42,020
and pretty soon that will be true of computers.

587
00:42:42,021 --> 00:42:44,760
that the only reason you have a computer is to be on the network,

588
00:42:44,880 --> 00:42:46,780
not to do word processing and spreadsheets.

589
00:42:46,880 --> 00:42:47,600
Those are incidental.

590
00:42:48,960 --> 00:42:51,100
At the start of the 21st century,

591
00:42:51,560 --> 00:42:53,000
the Internet is turning everything,

592
00:42:53,320 --> 00:42:55,220
from business to games to dating,

593
00:42:55,580 --> 00:42:58,020
into a series of keystrokes and mouse clicks.

594
00:42:58,640 --> 00:43:02,900
Everything the free enterprise system can put on sale is going online.

595
00:43:04,180 --> 00:43:06,500
Web pages can even turn one person's interest,

596
00:43:06,660 --> 00:43:07,620
no matter how unique,

597
00:43:07,980 --> 00:43:09,940
into a source of information for others.

598
00:43:09,941 --> 00:43:18,940
This online community called Netizens now communicates and connects around the globe, unfettered by boundaries and borders,

599
00:43:18,941 --> 00:43:25,940
all because of this great communications infrastructure that was built like a great cathedral, one brick at a time.

600
00:43:27,940 --> 00:43:32,940
This marvel of art and science is still very much under construction.

601
00:43:32,941 --> 00:43:38,300
To make something happen on the scale that it has worldwide, it really takes lots of

602
00:43:38,301 --> 00:43:40,700
people to see opportunity and go capitalize on it.

603
00:43:40,701 --> 00:43:47,400
The combination of email as we've traditionally known it, augmented by graphics and video

604
00:43:47,401 --> 00:43:53,220
and voice, is going to become the wave of the future.

605
00:43:53,221 --> 00:43:57,500
And it's going to be hard to tell the difference between voice communication and email, because

606
00:43:57,501 --> 00:44:04,500
if email can contain voice, then that will probably suffice. It's coming right now.

607
00:44:05,020 --> 00:44:10,100
What began as a million-dollar investment by the government in 1969 to link computers

608
00:44:10,101 --> 00:44:15,090
to share information was now, at the turn of the century, the future.

609
00:44:15,091 --> 00:44:16,320
At present, there

610
00:44:16,321 --> 00:44:23,320
are over 11 million domain names registered on the Internet and nearly 70 million websites.

611
00:44:23,321 --> 00:44:28,160
www.com and at are the new icons of the information age.

612
00:44:28,700 --> 00:44:31,400
With the enormous use of the Internet and the Web,

613
00:44:31,880 --> 00:44:34,240
innovation and evolution will continue,

614
00:44:34,820 --> 00:44:38,620
and it will reinvent communications of all kinds.

615
00:44:40,460 --> 00:44:44,300
I don't think we know yet just how important this is.

616
00:44:44,301 --> 00:44:46,960
Many of us believe that this is, in fact,

617
00:44:47,200 --> 00:44:50,200
the telecommunications infrastructure for the 21st century,

618
00:44:50,201 --> 00:44:54,180
that it will absorb and support all of the other media,

619
00:44:54,320 --> 00:44:56,140
telephony, television, radio,

620
00:44:56,480 --> 00:44:58,600
and of course conventional computer communications.

621
00:44:59,180 --> 00:45:01,400
But in some sense, it's still early days.

622
00:45:01,600 --> 00:45:04,960
Remember that this system is no older in 1999

623
00:45:04,961 --> 00:45:08,200
than the telephone system was in 1900.

624
00:45:08,460 --> 00:45:12,280
You know, the telephone's 123 years old.

625
00:45:12,460 --> 00:45:14,840
The Internet is only 30 years old.

626
00:45:15,060 --> 00:45:17,400
And it's got a lot of future ahead of it.

627
00:45:17,401 --> 00:45:21,780
So don't think for a moment that this little look at the history is going to capture it all,

628
00:45:21,880 --> 00:45:24,100
because it's happening right now, even as we sit here.

629
00:46:14,440 --> 00:46:15,640
topic

